2 CONT https://groups.google.com/forum/?fromgroups=#!topic/soc.genealogy.medieval/HrAqAioYXwU
2 CONT
2 CONT  Zetland Family History, by Sir Francis J Grant, published in 1907, there is a section on the Mouat of Garth (Delting) family.  The first mentioned is "Andrew Mowat of Hugoland, in Delting" who married secondly Lady Elsie Trond'sd
2 CONC aughter, with a son Axel Mowat, father of Catherine who married the Baron of Rosendale.  Elsie had a sister "Anna Trond'sdaughter with whom Bothwell eloped from Norway," and Anna "is stated by Norwegian genealogists to have been w
2 CONC idow of Jon Kaar of Gjerebvig, and Axel Fredricksen Fridag, lawman in Bergen.
2 CONT
2 CONT This would appear to be the family Thorleif is refering to, but then why are the sisters Trond'sdaughter and not Christoffer'sdaughter?   Bothwell was in Norway after his marriage to Mary Stuart.
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrew Mowat had five sons by his first wife, the fourth being Gilbert Mowat, born 1579, who was minister of Delting, and later, Northmavine parishes in Shetland.  There is no mention of his having been clan chief, but he was the 
2 CONC founder of the family of Mouat of Garth, who were major landowners in Shetland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Grant also mentions the family of Mowat of Balquholly.  The earliest is Patrick Mowat of Balquholly, witness to a charter in 1532, who was married to  Margaret Cheyne.  The next of the family was also Patrick Mowat, implied to be 
2 CONC son of the foregoing, of Balquholly and Freswick, married to Christian daughter of Walter Ogilvy of Boyne, and was served heir to his father in 1565.  He had three children: Magnus, his heir, James and Isabella.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is obviously plenty of space for other children in both these Balquholly families, and Barbara Sinclair and the Heppburn connection could be by second wives.
2 CONT
2 CONT It would be interesting to learn more.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Alan Beattie Coontin Kin in Shetland and Tracing my Sussex forebears
2 CONT
